{"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/SwiftStats\/documentation\/SwiftStats"]]},"kind":"article","abstract":[{"text":"Perform Student’s\/Welch’s t-tests, paired t-tests, Chi-Square goodness-of-fit tests, normality tests, and one-way ANOVA.","type":"text"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","anchor":"Overview","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiftStats"},{"text":" supports complete inferential statistics with numerical stability across extreme sample offsets, large degrees of freedom (","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"df >= 2e5"},{"text":" via Temme uniform asymptotic expansion), and sub-machine-epsilon upper tails.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":3,"text":"1. Two-Sample Welch \/ Student t-Test","type":"heading","anchor":"1-Two-Sample-Welch--Student-t-Test"},{"type":"codeListing","code":["import SwiftStats","","let groupA: [Double] = [12.1, 14.3, 11.8, 13.5, 15.0]","let groupB: [Double] = [9.8, 10.5, 11.2, 10.1, 9.4]","","let result = try Stats.tTest(sample1: groupA, sample2: groupB)","print(\"t-statistic: \\(result.statistic), p-value: \\(result.pValue)\")","if result.isSignificant {","    print(\"Statistically significant difference (p < 0.05)\")","}"],"syntax":"swift"},{"level":3,"text":"2. One-Way ANOVA","anchor":"2-One-Way-ANOVA","type":"heading"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let samples: [[Double]] = [groupA, groupB, [8.5, 9.0, 8.8]]","let anova = try Stats.oneWayANOVA(groups: samples)","print(\"F-statistic: \\(anova.fStatistic), p-value: \\(anova.pValue)\")"]},{"level":3,"text":"3. Chi-Square Goodness-of-Fit","anchor":"3-Chi-Square-Goodness-of-Fit","type":"heading"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Stats.chiSquareGoodnessOfFit(observed:expected:)"},{"text":" requires at least two categories, finite non-negative observed frequencies, and strictly positive finite expected frequencies. Upper-tail survival probabilities use direct Lentz continued fractions and Temme asymptotics (","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"a = df \/ 2 >= 1e5"},{"text":").","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let chi2 = try Stats.chiSquareGoodnessOfFit(","    observed: [20, 30, 50],","    expected: [25, 25, 50]",")","print(\"chi2 = \\(chi2.statistic), p = \\(chi2.pValue), df = \\(chi2.degreesOfFreedom)\")"]},{"text":"4. Normality Tests (Shapiro–Wilk & Lilliefors KS)","type":"heading","anchor":"4-Normality-Tests-Shapiro-Wilk--Lilliefors-KS","level":3},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Stats.shapiroWilk(_:)"},{"text":" implements Royston AS R94 for ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"3 <= n <= 5000"},{"type":"text","text":" with shift\/scale invariance ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"1e12"},{"type":"text","text":" offsets, subnormal to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"1e308"},{"type":"text","text":" dynamic range) and rejects non-finite or constant samples."}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Stats.kolmogorovSmirnov(_:)"},{"type":"text","text":" implements the Lilliefors normality test against sample-fitted normal parameters ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"n >= 5"},{"type":"text","text":") using Dallal–Wilkinson and Stephens calibrated p-values."}]}]}]},{"code":["let sample: [Double] = [2.1, 2.5, 2.7, 3.0, 3.1, 3.3, 3.8]","let sw = try Stats.shapiroWilk(sample)","let ks = try Stats.kolmogorovSmirnov(sample)","print(\"Shapiro-Wilk W = \\(sw.statistic), p = \\(sw.pValue)\")","print(\"Lilliefors D = \\(ks.statistic), p = \\(ks.pValue)\")"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/swiftstats\/hypothesistesting"]}],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","title":"Hypothesis Testing & ANOVA","modules":[{